From 48a61270c380ba41b19a44d739bbc0299fd626fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chromium Extend <noreply@localhost>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 20:32:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Ship uBlock Origin with the browser

Content blocking now works from the first launch rather than after a trip to
the extension store, which matters more here than on desktop: the store is
about to stop serving Manifest V2 at all.

The extension is registered through the external provider at kExternalPref, so
it installs on first run and is then the user's to keep. It can be disabled, it
can be removed, and it appears in the extensions list like anything installed
by hand. A component extension would have been simpler but could be neither
turned off nor seen, which is the wrong bargain for an ad blocker. Nothing is
pinned, so it takes no toolbar space.

Getting at the file needs a small loader. The usual route, an
external_extensions.json under DIR_EXTERNAL_EXTENSIONS, resolves to
DIR_MODULE/extensions on Android, which is the read-only library directory
inside the apk, so there is nowhere to write. Assets have no path of their own
either, so BareBundledExtensionsLoader copies the crx out of the apk on first
run and hands the installer a real file. The copy is named after the version,
so shipping a newer one lands beside the old rather than being mistaken for it,
and failures are non-fatal: a missing asset means the extension is not offered,
not that startup breaks.

The crx itself is not in this patch. It is a 4MB signed third party binary, and
base64 in a patch would be unreviewable, so it lives in the Bare repository
under third_party/ublock_origin and is copied in before building. See the
README beside this BUILD.gn.

uBlock Origin is GPL-3.0. It is redistributed unmodified, keeping its original
signature and extension id, so a copy installed from the store is recognised as
the same extension rather than a duplicate. Its licence text travels inside the
crx; provenance, checksum and the corresponding source are recorded alongside
the file.
